WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:03.110
Donald Trump's impeachment trial starts today.

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:07.620
Now we're going to talk about in a bit after
the break, what is the argument that Donald

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.370
Trump is making about the impeachment?

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.160
What is the argument that his lawyers will
make about the impeachment?

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.280
But first, let's talk about what should happen
in this impeachment.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.400
If this was a fair world where actions had
consequences, even if you're the president,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.569
there should be probably a secret ballot for
impeachment so that senators would not be

00:00:25.569 --> 00:00:31.309
bullied into voting to acquit merely to avoid
what might be the political consequences.

00:00:31.309 --> 00:00:34.540
There should be a secret ballot and there
should be a unanimous conviction of Donald

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.300
Trump based on the evidence that we've seen.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.610
Now, there's a really good article, an op
ed in the Hill, I hope.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.720
Pronouncing his last name correctly, it's
Douglas Mique, CMYK.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:49.910
I hope I'm pronouncing that correctly, and
Douglas points out that the entire argument

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:54.560
that will take up the first day in today's
Senate impeachment trial, which is is the

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:01.840
impeachment trial even constitutional, that
is not the duty of the Senate to determine

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.420
the duty of the Senate is not to determine
whether this is constitutional.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.649
First of all, it is.

00:01:07.649 --> 00:01:15.429
And secondly, why would you put many non scholars,
nonlegal professionals in a position of deciding

00:01:15.429 --> 00:01:17.049
about the constitutionality of something?

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:18.820
It really doesn't make sense.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:25.549
What jurors in the Senate should be determining
is whether Donald Trump could reasonably foresee

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:32.149
that the consequences of him saying it's all
rigged for a month or two months.

00:01:32.149 --> 00:01:33.409
Let's go down to the capital.

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:34.930
We're marching down to the capital.

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:38.549
Rudy Giuliani, you know, trial by combat,
all of this stuff.

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:44.579
Could it was it foreseeable that the uncontested
reality of what took place was something that

00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:47.159
that was likely to take place?

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:50.250
That is what the determination ultimately
needs to be.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:55.850
Donald Trump doesn't have the law on his side
when it comes to avoiding impeachment because

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.460
of the constitutional issue.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:01.310
But in any case, that's not really what the
Senate should be deciding.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:05.909
And there is really no disagreement about
what took place on January six.

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:11.740
This Douglas Kmiec points out, even Trump's
defense team acknowledges the facts of what

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:18.070
took place on January 6th, which was a violent
insurrection attempt at the U.S. Capitol.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:22.260
So then it becomes a question of really common
sense.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:23.790
Remember, this is not a court of law.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:30.280
The Senate was was it reasonable for Donald
Trump to foresee what took place?

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.780
And it's very hard to imagine that the answer
is no.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.751
Donald Trump was reportedly gleeful in the
early hours of the insurrection, Donald Trump

00:02:38.751 --> 00:02:44.620
reportedly resisted sending law enforcement,
which would have quelled or limited what took

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:45.730
place.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:51.090
And only when Donald Trump was convinced that
the political consequences could be too grave.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:56.470
Did he come out and give that mealy mouthed
sort of statement about, oh, we've got to

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:58.500
be peaceful in all of this stuff.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.050
So that's what should happen.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:05.230
Trump won't be convicted and the voting won't
be by secret ballot.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:09.110
I was I was actually thinking, imagine that
there was a secret ballot where instead of

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:14.320
voting out loud publicly, senators voted secretly.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.010
Imagine that you end up with 80 votes to convict.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:22.360
So, you know, 30 Republicans voted to convict,
but none of them are willing to admit it.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.470
You know what would happen, right?

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:28.620
They would, again, start with voter fraud,
supposedly 80 votes to convict.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:34.070
And we can't find a single Republican willing
to say, yes, I voted to convict.

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:35.070
But you know what?

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.580
It's going to be a moot point because they're
not going to convict.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:45.940
And then it will be up to voters to determine
whether they care enough to force consequences

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.850
upon these Republican senators in the 2022
election.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:54.190
Now, as a as a reminder, when we talk about
representative democracy, a majority of Americans

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.730
say that Donald Trump should be convicted
and barred from holding office in the future.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:02.260
Fifty six percent of Americans we looked at
that ABC poll yesterday.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:07.180
Trump's lawyers are saying that the entire
trial is merely political theater and the

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:13.390
arguments that they are making in their early
legal brief and we will see today and tomorrow

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:17.170
more so how they actually make this case a
reality.

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:25.010
They are both saying that the entire process
is unconstitutional and that the House impeachment

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:27.840
vote was extraordinarily biased.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.530
And they are going to say that this is censorship
and it's an attempt to silence Donald Trump.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.350
And of course, that's not what it is.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.750
Donald Trump was able to say whatever the
hell he wanted.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:42.400
And now there may be consequences to him saying
that, remember, free speech doesn't mean freedom

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.790
from all consequences to your speech.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.220
One other little note.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.730
Apparently, Trump's defense team is planning
to show clips of what they say are examples

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:54.550
of Democrats urging violence from last year.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:57.760
So I think that there's going to be a lot
of interesting moments during this.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:01.670
But ultimately, we are going to get the political
theater is going to be Trump's defense.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.990
We are going to get an acquittal.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.630
And then ultimately we will see if the American
people decide that these Republican senators

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:09.400
need to be held accountable.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.000
The American public often has a short memory.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:18.290
So I am not particularly optimistic that Republican
senators who have prejudged this and already

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.410
know they are going to acquit will be held
accountable.

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:24.060
It would be nice if they were, but I don't
anticipate that.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:30.160
And we will be live streaming the impeachment
on the David Pakman show, YouTube Twitch and

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:31.350
Facebook channels.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:32.710
I hope you'll join me.

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:38.250
But in the meantime, let me know via Twitter
what you expect to see in this trial.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:06:29.449
You can find me on.

00:06:29.449 --> 00:06:56.669
Twitter at David Pakman

